Proposed human-doc change for issue #131 — browser token storage.

This patch is NOT applied by the implementation step. ADR-031 and
context-human/concepts/ux.md are human-owned; this file records the proposed
wording change for review through the human docs review process. It updates the
current "the browser stores only the operator's preferences" statement to say the
browser stores the operator's preferences AND the bearer token until the desktop
wrapper supplies keychain storage, and that the token uses a separate storage key
and is never displayed.

To review: read the two diffs below. To apply after approval, run from the repo
root: `git apply context-agent/proposed-human-docs/issue-131-browser-token-storage.patch`

diff --git a/context-human/adrs/adr-031-web-client-architecture.md b/context-human/adrs/adr-031-web-client-architecture.md
--- a/context-human/adrs/adr-031-web-client-architecture.md
+++ b/context-human/adrs/adr-031-web-client-architecture.md
@@ -24,3 +24,5 @@
 **The client is one web app that talks only to the control plane's HTTP and SSE API. It keeps no run or
-project state of its own. The browser stores only the operator's preferences. The desktop app wraps the
+project state of its own. The browser stores the operator's preferences and the bearer token until the
+desktop wrapper supplies keychain storage. Preferences and the bearer token use separate browser storage
+keys; the token is secret material, kept under its own key and never displayed. The desktop app wraps the
 same web app and adds device features such as a keychain, and a mobile mode shows a subset.**
diff --git a/context-human/concepts/ux.md b/context-human/concepts/ux.md
--- a/context-human/concepts/ux.md
+++ b/context-human/concepts/ux.md
@@ -36,3 +36,5 @@
 The client only talks to the control plane. It holds no run or project state of its own, and the browser
-keeps only the operator's preferences. This is what lets a run move between devices.
+keeps the operator's preferences and the bearer token until the desktop wrapper supplies keychain storage.
+Preferences and the bearer token use separate browser storage keys; the token is secret material, kept
+under its own key and never displayed. This is what lets a run move between devices.

